1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,047
Northstar.

2
00:00:01,047 --> 00:00:06,417
Fast, exact decoding for Cohere's mixture of experts model North, on a single H100.

3
00:00:06,417 --> 00:00:11,851
This is a short tour of what we built, what we measured, and where it does not help.

4
00:00:12,850 --> 00:00:18,200
North is a large mixture of experts model, and decoding produces one token per forward pass.

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:23,084
A stock graphed PyTorch decode leaves the GPU waiting between many small operations.

6
00:00:23,084 --> 00:00:27,446
Speculation usually either changes the output, or costs more than it saves.

7
00:00:27,446 --> 00:00:30,062
We wanted speed with exactly the same tokens.

8
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:36,033
The pieces are a Triton decode executor, with attention and the mixture of experts branches

9
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:37,672
running on concurrent streams.

10
00:00:37,672 --> 00:00:42,044
Batch invariant kernels, so output does not depend on what else is in the batch.

11
00:00:42,044 --> 00:00:46,525
Prompt lookup speculation that is checked against the executor's own plain decode.

12
00:00:46,525 --> 00:00:49,421
And a serving engine with continuous batching on top.

13
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:51,725
One launch, one H100.

14
00:00:51,725 --> 00:00:57,316
At a two thousand token context, the graphed PyTorch baseline takes 10.70 milliseconds per

15
00:00:57,316 --> 00:00:57,688
token.

16
00:00:57,688 --> 00:01:00,298
The exact version two executor takes 3.12.

17
00:01:00,298 --> 00:01:06,137
The older non exact executor sits in between, and the first exact version is slower than that.

18
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:12,465
That is 3.4 times faster per token, and 3.4 times at an eight thousand token context.

19
00:01:12,465 --> 00:01:18,228
In live decoding that is about 319 tokens per second, with the same tokens as the executor's

20
00:01:18,228 --> 00:01:19,293
own plain decode.

21
00:01:20,290 --> 00:01:27,498
Against stock vLLM zero point three three, in the same container, on the same prompts.

22
00:01:27,498 --> 00:01:34,036
With one user we reach 310 tokens per second against 258, which is 1.20 times.

23
00:01:34,036 --> 00:01:39,316
With thirty two users, 2326 against 2213, a lead of 1.05 times.

24
00:01:39,316 --> 00:01:44,513
Thirty two users together get 7.5 times the throughput of one.

25
00:01:45,510 --> 00:01:51,567
A different experiment: embedding small trainable workers inside one frozen North forward pass.

26
00:01:51,567 --> 00:01:57,113
On a state tracking task with four entities, frozen North scores 0.14, close to chance.

27
00:01:57,113 --> 00:01:59,153
With an adapter it reaches 0.98.

28
00:01:59,153 --> 00:02:04,254
But the fancier private slots design, at 0.92, does not beat the simple adapter.

29
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:06,607
Where it does not help.

30
00:02:06,607 --> 00:02:11,918
Against vLLM the lead is small from eight users up, and around sixteen requests per second

31
00:02:11,918 --> 00:02:14,161
under arrivals we are slightly slower.

32
00:02:14,161 --> 00:02:17,879
Self speculation, the original thesis, never beat plain decode.

33
00:02:17,879 --> 00:02:23,426
North tuning its own kernel produced only a small gain, a first sign rather than an explosion.

34
00:02:23,426 --> 00:02:28,147
And all of this is one model, one GPU type, greedy decoding and single launches.

35
00:02:29,150 --> 00:02:34,923
What holds up: an exact decoder that is 3.4 times faster than graphed PyTorch on one H100

36
00:02:34,923 --> 00:02:41,021
ahead of stock vLLM from one to thirty two users in the same container, and every claim in the

37
00:02:41,021 --> 00:02:44,135
repo labeled as measured, inferred or simulated.

38
00:02:45,140 --> 00:02:50,221
The code, the raw results, and the full results write up are all in the repo.

